    "summary": "High‑energy Portland show with monster Truckin' jam and soaring China>Rider",
    "blurb": "The band rides a relentless groove through an explosive second set, highlighted by a massive Truckin' jam and the classic China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue. Jerry’s guitar is razor‑sharp, Phil’s bass sits front‑and‑center, and Keith delivers an epic piano solo on The Promised Land. Overall the performance is tight, psychedelic, and consistently high‑octane.",
    "review": "May 19, 1974 at Portland Memorial Coliseum captures the Dead in peak form. After a solid first set that builds momentum with Mississippi Half‑Step Uptown Toodeloo and Mexicali Blues, the second set erupts into one of the era’s most memorable stretches: The Promised Land launches straight into a soaring Bertha, segueing into an expansive Weather Report Suite that showcases Phil’s “sick lead bass” and Keith’s tasteful keyboards. The centerpiece is a monster Truckin' jam that rolls into a Mind Left Body Jam before a funky Not Fade Away → Goin’ Down The Road Feeling Bad transition, keeping the crowd on its feet until the final U.S. Blues encore. Across every recording, reviewers note Jerry’s “on‑fire” lead work, Keith’s piano duel with Garcia, and Bill Kreutzmann’s propulsive drumming, making this a must‑listen snapshot of the band’s mid‑70s power.\n\nThe soundboard sources are uniformly strong; the AJ capture (gd1974-05-19.sbd.aj.203.sbeok.shnf) is singled out for its crystal‑clear mix and front‑center Phil bass, while Charlie Miller’s fixes provide excellent clarity despite occasional vocal drop‑outs. For fans who want both fidelity and the full energy of the night, the AJ SBD is the definitive pick.",
